rdb.library/AddFileSystem                            rdb.library/AddFileSystem

   NAME
	AddFileSystem -- Keeps a handler's load file in a handle's table, for the partitions of one DosType.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddFileSystem(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, dos_type: u32, code: [*]const u8, size: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -60.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with a table (RDBF_FOUND, or after InitRDB).
	- `dos_type`: the de_DosType of the partitions that are to run it.
	- `code`, `size`: the handler's load file, as it is in HANDLERS:.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK. RDBERR_NORDB for a handle with no table; RDBERR_FILESYSTEM
	for code that is not a load file for this machine holding a handler's
	ROM tag; RDBERR_NOMEM.

   BEHAVIOR
	The code is copied and a new RDBFileSystem goes at the end of the
	handle's list, with a FileSysHeaderBlock for `dos_type` whose version
	is the handler's own: its tag's rt_Version, and the revision its ID
	string gives. One kept for the same DosType before is taken out and
	freed, as RemFileSystem would. RDBF_CHANGED is set; nothing reaches
	the disk before WriteRDB, which answers RDBERR_FULL when the code does
	not fit in the table's area even grown up to the first partition.

	Once written, the partitions of `dos_type` on this disk run the code
	when the ROM has no handler for the DosType or an older one
	(`sdk.dos.filesystems.storedWins`): mounted by MountPartition, by the
	device that reads the table when the medium comes in, or at boot by
	the flash disk's driver.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`code` stays the caller's; the copy is the handle's until WriteRDB has
	written it, or the file system is taken out.

   NOTES
	The code is for the machine it was built for: a medium given the code
	on one chip runs it only there.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`RemFileSystem`, `NextFileSystem`, `WriteRDB`, `MountPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    // `code` holds HANDLERS:posfs-handler, read with Open and Read.
	    const err = rb.AddFileSystem(handle, sdk.dos.posfs.ID_POSFS_DISK, code, size);
	    if (err == rdb.RDBERR_OK) _ = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/AddGPTPartition                        rdb.library/AddGPTPartition

   NAME
	AddGPTPartition -- Adds a partition to a handle's GPT, over blocks no other partition has.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddGPTPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, entry: *const gpt.Entry, err: ?*i32) ?*rdb.GPTPartition

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -80.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB with a sound GPT, or after InitGPT.
	- `entry`: the partition: its type (`partition_type_guid`), its first
	  and last block (`starting_lba`, `ending_lba`; both 0 for the largest
	  free run), its attributes and its name. A `unique_partition_guid` of
	  all zeroes gets a new random one.
	- `err`: where the reason goes when the answer is null, or null.

   RESULT
	The new partition, on the handle's list; `err` is then RDBERR_OK.
	Null for RDBERR_NOGPT, a handle without a sound GPT; RDBERR_GPT, a
	type of all zeroes or a container where the GPT has one; RDBERR_RANGE,
	blocks outside the usable ones, the wrong way round, another
	partition's, or a first block not on a multiple of
	`gpt.alignment_bytes` - or, for 0 and 0, no aligned block free;
	RDBERR_FULL, every entry used; RDBERR_NOMEM.

   BEHAVIOR
	The partition gets the lowest entry number no partition has. A
	container (`gpt.TYPE_CONTAINER`) becomes the handle's window: RDBF_CONTAINER
	is set, `container` is its first block and `geometry` counts its
	blocks, and InitRDB makes a table in it. Only the handle changes, and
	RDBF_GPTCHANGED is set; WriteGPT puts the partition on the disk.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition is the handle's, freed by RemGPTPartition or CloseRDB.

   NOTES
	Nothing is written on the partition's blocks: what was there stays
	until a file system is formatted on it.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`InitGPT`, `RemGPTPartition`, `WriteGPT`, `MountGPTPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    var entry: gpt.Entry = .{ .partition_type_guid = gpt.TYPE_BASIC_DATA };
	    gpt.setName(&entry, "Shared");
	    var err: i32 = 0;
	    const part = rb.AddGPTPartition(handle, &entry, &err) orelse return err;
rdb.library/AddPartition                              rdb.library/AddPartition

   NAME
	AddPartition -- Adds a partition to a handle's table, over cylinders no other partition has.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, name: [*:0]const u8, low_cyl: u32, high_cyl: u32, dos_type: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with a table (RDBF_FOUND, or after InitRDB).
	- `name`: its drive name, without the colon (`DH1`): 1 to
	  MAX_DEVICE_NAME characters, no colon or slash, no other partition's.
	- `low_cyl`, `high_cyl`: its first and last cylinder, both its own;
	  0 and 0 for the longest run of cylinders no partition has.
	- `dos_type`: the file system it is for (`ID_FLASHFS_DISK`, a FAT
	  handler's), which picks its handler when it is mounted.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK; RDBERR_NORDB for a handle with no table; RDBERR_NAME for a
	name it may not have; RDBERR_RANGE for cylinders outside the table's
	usable ones (`lo_cylinder` to `hi_cylinder`), the wrong way round or
	another partition's, and for 0 and 0 when no cylinder is free;
	RDBERR_FULL when the table's own blocks have no room for another
	PartitionBlock; RDBERR_NOMEM. The table is unchanged by a failure.

   BEHAVIOR
	The new partition goes at the end of the list, and FindPartition with
	the name finds it. Its environment is filled in for the table's
	geometry: blocks of the table's size, one surface, a track of a
	cylinder's blocks, 32 buffers in the memory the medium asks for, and
	boot priority 0. Its flags are none - not bootable, mounted at boot -
	and are the program's to set, as is any field of the environment,
	before WriteRDB. RDBF_CHANGED is set.

	Only the table in memory changes: nothing is written, and nothing on
	the cylinders is touched, now or by WriteRDB. A file system goes on the
	partition with Format, once it is mounted.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition is the handle's, freed by RemPartition or CloseRDB.

   NOTES
	The flash disk's table is read at boot, so a partition written there
	with WriteRDB is mounted at the next one.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`FindPartition`, `RemPartition`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.AddPartition(handle, "DH1", 0, 0, flashfs.ID_FLASHFS_DISK) != rdb.RDBERR_OK) return;
	    rb.FindPartition(handle, "DH1").?.block.environment.boot_pri = -10;
	    const err = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/CloseRDB                                      rdb.library/CloseRDB

   NAME
	CloseRDB -- Lets go of a handle: its partition nodes, its GPT's, its buffer and the unit it has open.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseRDB(base: *RDBBase, handle: ?*rdb.RDBHandle) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: what OpenRDB answered, or null.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	Nothing is written: a table changed and not written with WriteRDB, or
	a GPT not written with WriteGPT, is dropped, and the disk keeps what
	it has. Null does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, CloseDevice may.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the handle.

   OWNERSHIP
	The handle and every RDBPartition, RDBFileSystem and GPTPartition of
	it are freed; a pointer to any of them is no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRDB`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    const handle = rb.OpenRDB("flash.device", 0, null) orelse return;
	    defer rb.CloseRDB(handle);
rdb.library/FindPartition                            rdb.library/FindPartition

   NAME
	FindPartition -- The partition of a handle that has a name, in any case.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FindPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, name: [*:0]const u8) ?*rdb.RDBPartition

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `name`: the drive name, without the colon (`DH0`).

   RESULT
	The partition, or null when none is called that.

   BEHAVIOR
	Names are compared without regard to case, as dos compares device
	names.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition stays the handle's.

   NOTES
	A name with a colon is no partition's: none may hold one.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`NextPartition`, `AddPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    const part = rb.FindPartition(handle, "DH1") orelse return;
	    part.block.flags |= hardblocks.PBFF_BOOTABLE;
rdb.library/InitGPT                                        rdb.library/InitGPT

   NAME
	InitGPT -- Puts a fresh GPT for the whole medium in a handle, with no partitions.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn InitGPT(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle) i32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -76.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with any table or none.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK, or RDBERR_RANGE for a medium too small for the GPT's two
	copies and one partition aligned between them.

   BEHAVIOR
	The header is made for the medium as TD_GETGEOMETRY tells it: the
	medium's own block size, room for `gpt.entries_made` entries of
	`gpt.entry_bytes` after it in block 2 and before the copy in the last
	block, every block between them usable, and a new random disk GUID.
	Partitions AddGPTPartition makes start on a multiple of
	`gpt.alignment_bytes`.

	Whatever the handle held goes: the GPT partitions, a RigidDiskBlock
	table with its partitions and file systems, the window onto a
	container. The handle then has RDBF_GPT and RDBF_GPTCHANGED, and no
	table until a container is added and InitRDB makes one in it. Only the
	handle changes; WriteGPT puts the GPT on the disk, and until then the
	disk keeps what it had.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partitions and file systems are freed; a pointer to any of them is
	no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	A GPT that replaces a RigidDiskBlock table over the whole disk has
	that table's blocks written over by WriteGPT.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddGPTPartition`, `WriteGPT`, `RemGPT`, `InitRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.InitGPT(handle) != rdb.RDBERR_OK) return;
	    var entry: gpt.Entry = .{ .partition_type_guid = gpt.TYPE_CONTAINER };
	    gpt.setName(&entry, "PowerOS");
	    _ = rb.AddGPTPartition(handle, &entry, null) orelse return;
	    _ = rb.InitRDB(handle);
rdb.library/InitRDB                                        rdb.library/InitRDB

   NAME
	InitRDB -- Puts a fresh RigidDiskBlock for the whole medium in a handle, with no partitions.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn InitRDB(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with a table or without.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK; RDBERR_RANGE for a medium with too few blocks for the
	table and one partition (RDB_LOCATION_LIMIT and two more), or a
	container that does not start on a multiple of the table's cylinder;
	RDBERR_GPT for a disk with a GPT and no container in it, whose other
	partitions the table would be written over.

   BEHAVIOR
	The table is made for the medium as TD_GETGEOMETRY tells it: blocks of
	the medium's own size; a cylinder of one block, or on a medium of 2^32
	blocks or more, of the fewest blocks - 2, 4, 8 and so on - that leave
	fewer than 2^32 cylinders; and the first RDB_LOCATION_LIMIT blocks
	kept for the table - the RigidDiskBlock and the PartitionBlocks, which
	WriteRDB places in them - so every cylinder from the first past them
	to the end may be a partition's. It says nothing of its vendor or
	product; those fields are the program's to fill in.

	A medium whose first block is a PC's partition table, as OpenRDB found
	it - a card as it is sold, or as a PC formatted it - keeps that block:
	the table's area starts at the second, which is still among the blocks
	the RigidDiskBlock is looked for in, and the PC goes on seeing its
	partitions. A partition of the table may then name the same blocks as
	one of the PC's, so the one volume is the PC's and this system's.

	On a disk with a GPT the table is the container's (RDBF_CONTAINER):
	made for the container's blocks, which `geometry` counts, from its
	first block on. A disk with a GPT and no container gets no table:
	AddGPTPartition adds a container, or RemGPT takes the GPT out of the
	handle for a table over the whole disk.

	Every partition and every file system is taken off its list and
	freed, and the chains of bad blocks and drive init code the disk had
	are forgotten: the new table has none. Only the handle changes, and
	RDBF_CHANGED is set; WriteRDB puts it on the disk, and until then the
	disk keeps what it had.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partitions and file systems are freed; a pointer to any of them
	is no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	A partition begins and ends on a cylinder, so on a medium of 2^32
	blocks or more it begins and ends on a multiple of the cylinder's
	blocks; the few blocks past the last whole cylinder are no
	partition's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRDB`, `AddPartition`, `WriteRDB`, `InitGPT`, `RemGPT`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.InitRDB(handle) != rdb.RDBERR_OK) return;
	    _ = rb.AddPartition(handle, "SD0", 0, 0, dos_type);
	    const err = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/MountGPTPartition                    rdb.library/MountGPTPartition

   NAME
	MountGPTPartition -- Puts a partition of a disk's GPT on dos's list as a device node, so that the file system on it can be started.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn MountGPTPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, partition: *rdb.GPTPartition, name: [*:0]const u8, mounted: ?*rdb.MountName, flags: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -72.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with RDBF_GPT.
	- `partition`: one of the handle's GPT partitions, not the container.
	- `name`: what the drive is to be called, without the colon (`SD0`,
	  `SD0P3`).
	- `mounted`: where the name the node got goes, or null.
	- `flags`: RDBMF_START to start the handler before the call returns;
	  0 leaves it to the drive's first use.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK when the node is on the list, and `mounted` holds its name.
	RDBERR_NOGPT for a handle without a GPT; RDBERR_FILESYSTEM for the
	container, or a partition whose first block holds no file system this
	machine reads or one that cannot go on this medium; RDBERR_NAME for a
	name that is empty, too long, holds a colon or a slash, or is taken
	with every dot that fits; RDBERR_RANGE for a partition a cylinder
	cannot be found for (one that starts on an odd block of a disk of 2^32
	blocks or more); RDBERR_IO when its first block cannot be read;
	RDBERR_CHANGED when the medium in the unit is not the one the GPT was
	read from; RDBERR_MOUNTED when a device node on the list already opens
	some of its blocks; RDBERR_NOMEM. The list is unchanged by a failure.

   BEHAVIOR
	The partition's first block says what it holds: a posfs label gives
	posfs (`POS\0`), the boot sector of a FAT32 or exFAT volume FAT
	(`MSD\0`); the handler is the one that DosType asks for. The node opens
	the handle's device and unit, its environment names the partition's
	blocks and no more, with the medium's block size and memory, and it is
	called `name`, with a dot added while dos's list has a device or an
	assign of the name, as MountPartition does.

	A node of the same unit whose area shares a block with the partition's
	stops it, as it stops MountPartition: a drive over the whole medium,
	or the partition mounted already.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the device, for the dos list's lock, and with
	  RDBMF_START for the handler.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held, nor the dos list.
	- Process: a Task will do; a Process with RDBMF_START.

   OWNERSHIP
	The node is the list's, for the system's life; the handle and the
	partition stay the caller's.

   NOTES
	`gpt.ATTR_NO_AUTOMOUNT` is the caller's to honour, as PBFF_NOMOUNT is
	MountPartition's: a medium's partitions are mounted when it comes in
	without the ones that have it. The container's partitions are mounted
	with MountPartition.

	Once a partition is mounted the library stays in memory for the
	system's life.

   BUGS
	A FAT12 or FAT16 volume - a small EFI System partition is often one -
	is not one the FAT handler reads, and is not mounted.

   SEE ALSO
	`NextGPTPartition`, `MountPartition`, `OpenRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    var name: rdb.MountName = undefined;
	    const part = rb.NextGPTPartition(handle, null) orelse return;
	    if (rb.MountGPTPartition(handle, part, "SD0", &name, rdb.RDBMF_START) == rdb.RDBERR_OK) {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "mounted as %s:\n", .{@as([*:0]const u8, &name)});
	    }
rdb.library/MountPartition                          rdb.library/MountPartition

   NAME
	MountPartition -- Puts a partition on dos's list as a device node, so that its file system can be started.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn MountPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, partition: *rdb.RDBPartition, name: ?*rdb.MountName, flags: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.2. LVO -52.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `partition`: one of the handle's partitions.
	- `name`: where the name the node got goes, without the colon, or
	  null.
	- `flags`: RDBMF_START to start the handler before the call returns;
	  0 leaves it to the drive's first use.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK when the node is on the list, and `name` holds its name.
	RDBERR_FILESYSTEM for a DosType no handler serves and the table brings
	no code for, or whose file system cannot go on this medium or inside a
	partition (`MSH`, `POS\1`, which want a whole medium); RDBERR_IO when
	the table's code cannot be read; RDBERR_NAME for a partition name that is
	empty or too long, or one taken on the list with every dot that fits;
	RDBERR_CHANGED when the medium in the unit is not the one the table
	was read from; RDBERR_MOUNTED when a device node on the list already
	opens some of the partition's blocks; RDBERR_RANGE for a partition of
	a container that does not start on a multiple of the partition's
	cylinder, or that reaches past the cylinders 32 bits count;
	RDBERR_NOMEM for no memory. The list is unchanged by a failure.

   BEHAVIOR
	The node is named as the partition is. Where the list has a device or
	an assign of that name, a dot is added and the name tried again - `DH0`,
	`DH0.`, `DH0..` - as far as MAX_DEVICE_NAME characters, so a second
	disk whose table names its partitions as the first's gets drives of
	its own. The node opens the handle's device and unit with the
	partition's device flags, its environment is a copy of the
	partition's - with the container's cylinders added for a table in a
	GPT's container (RDBF_CONTAINER), so it names the partition's blocks
	on the disk - and its handler is the one its DosType asks for: the
	flash file system's for `FLS\0`, posfs's for `POS\0`, fat-handler for
	`MSD\0`. Where the ROM has that handler, the node gets its code as the
	entry dos starts (`sdk.dos.filesystems.romEntry`), so the drive runs
	the ROM's, not a file of that name in HANDLERS:.

	Where the handle's table keeps a file system for the DosType, its
	code is what the node runs when the ROM has no handler for the DosType
	or an older one (`sdk.dos.filesystems.storedWins`) - HANDLERS: plays
	no part in it. The code is copied into memory for the node, or the
	copy a node of the same unit got for the same DosType and version is
	shared; dos loads it when the handler is first started, and starts the
	handler its DosType names when the code does not load - by name then,
	the ROM's code is not put in such a node. A DosType that
	names no handler is mounted when the table brings its code.

	A node of the same unit whose area shares a block with the
	partition's is the partition's already, or a drive over the whole
	medium, or one over cylinders the table has since given another
	partition: in each case a second file system on those blocks would
	wreck the first, so nothing is mounted. Devices are compared by what
	they are called, the part of the name after its last `/` or `:`:
	`DEVS:usb/disk.device` is `disk.device`.

	With RDBMF_START the handler is started, which reads the partition and
	puts its volume on the list. A handler that cannot be started leaves
	the node there all the same, and the drive's first use says why.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the dos list's lock, and with RDBMF_START for the
	  handler.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held, nor the dos list.
	- Process: a Task will do; a Process with RDBMF_START.

   OWNERSHIP
	The node is the list's, for the system's life, and so is the copy of
	the table's code; the handle and the partition stay the caller's, and
	may be closed and freed at once.

   NOTES
	Mount what WriteRDB has put on the disk: a partition only in the
	handle is mounted all the same, but the disk does not have it at the
	next start. PBFF_NOMOUNT is the caller's to honour: it says a
	partition is left alone when a whole table is mounted, and
	MountPartition mounts what it is given.

	The node names its handler by a string the library holds, so once a
	partition is mounted the library stays in memory for the system's
	life.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRDB`, `FindPartition`, `WriteRDB`, `AddFileSystem`

   EXAMPLES
	    var name: rdb.MountName = undefined;
	    const part = rb.FindPartition(handle, "DH1") orelse return;
	    if (rb.MountPartition(handle, part, &name, rdb.RDBMF_START) == rdb.RDBERR_OK) {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "mounted as %s:\n", .{@as([*:0]const u8, &name)});
	    }
rdb.library/NextFileSystem                          rdb.library/NextFileSystem

   NAME
	NextFileSystem -- The file system after another on a handle's list of the table's file systems, or the first.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn NextFileSystem(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, previous: ?*rdb.RDBFileSystem) ?*rdb.RDBFileSystem

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -56.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `previous`: a file system of this handle, or null for the first.

   RESULT
	The next file system, or null past the last (or for a table that keeps
	none).

   BEHAVIOR
	The order is the list's: the disk's chain of FileSysHeaderBlocks as it
	was read, then what AddFileSystem added, which is the order WriteRDB
	writes the chain in. A header on the disk whose chain of code breaks
	is not on the list, and the handle is marked RDBF_DAMAGED.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The file system stays the handle's.

   NOTES
	RemFileSystem, and AddFileSystem for the same DosType, free the node
	just answered: take the next one first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddFileSystem`, `RemFileSystem`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    var file_system = rb.NextFileSystem(handle, null);
	    while (file_system) |fs| : (file_system = rb.NextFileSystem(handle, fs)) {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "%08lx version %u.%u\n", .{ fs.header.dos_type, fs.header.version >> 16, fs.header.version & 0xFFFF });
	    }
rdb.library/NextGPTPartition                      rdb.library/NextGPTPartition

   NAME
	NextGPTPartition -- Gives the GPT partition after another on a handle's list, or the first.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn NextGPTPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, previous: ?*rdb.GPTPartition) ?*rdb.GPTPartition

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -68.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `previous`: a partition of the handle's GPT, or null for the first.

   RESULT
	The next partition, or null after the last, and on a handle without
	RDBF_GPT.

   BEHAVIOR
	The list is in the order of the GPT's entries, by `number`; unused
	entries have no node. The container is on it, as a partition of the
	type `gpt.TYPE_CONTAINER`.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition stays the handle's.

   NOTES
	Taking `previous` off the list in the walk ends it: hold on to the
	next one first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRDB`, `MountGPTPartition`, `AddGPTPartition`, `NextPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    var name: [gpt.name_units:0]u8 = undefined;
	    var part = rb.NextGPTPartition(handle, null);
	    while (part) |p| : (part = rb.NextGPTPartition(handle, p)) {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "%u: %s\n", .{ p.number, gpt.nameText(&p.entry, &name).ptr });
	    }
rdb.library/NextPartition                            rdb.library/NextPartition

   NAME
	NextPartition -- The partition after another on a handle's list, or the first.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn NextPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, previous: ?*rdb.RDBPartition) ?*rdb.RDBPartition

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `previous`: a partition of this handle, or null for the first.

   RESULT
	The next partition, or null past the last (or for a handle with none).

   BEHAVIOR
	The order is the list's: the disk's chain as it was read, then what
	AddPartition added, which is the order WriteRDB writes the chain in.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition stays the handle's.

   NOTES
	RemPartition on the partition just answered ends the walk with it:
	take the next one first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`FindPartition`, `AddPartition`, `RemPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    var part = rb.NextPartition(handle, null);
	    while (part) |p| : (part = rb.NextPartition(handle, p)) {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "%s\n", .{rdb.partitionName(p)});
	    }
rdb.library/OpenRDB                                        rdb.library/OpenRDB

   NAME
	OpenRDB -- Opens a unit of a block device and reads what the disk says about itself into a handle.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenRDB(base: *RDBBase, device: [*:0]const u8, unit: u32, err: ?*i32) ?*rdb.RDBHandle

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `device`: the exec device, `flash.device` or `sdcard.device` or any
	  other that answers trackdisk's commands.
	- `unit`: its unit.
	- `err`: where the reason goes when the answer is null, or null.

   RESULT
	The handle, with the unit open; `err` is then RDBERR_OK. Null when the
	device or unit cannot be opened, answers no TD_GETGEOMETRY, has no
	medium in it or blocks smaller than 256 bytes (RDBERR_DEVICE), when
	the medium went or changed while its blocks were read (RDBERR_CHANGED),
	or for no memory (RDBERR_NOMEM).

   BEHAVIOR
	The first RDB_LOCATION_LIMIT blocks are searched for a sound
	RigidDiskBlock - the lowest is the table, as every reader of the disk
	takes it - and its chain of PartitionBlocks is read into one
	RDBPartition each, on `partitions` in the chain's order. The handle's
	flags say what was found:
	- RDBF_FOUND: a table, in `rdb`, read from block `block`.
	- RDBF_DAMAGED: the partition chain stops at a block that is not a
	  sound PartitionBlock, or comes round to itself; the partitions
	  before it are on the list.
	- RDBF_FOREIGN: a RigidDiskBlock written for another block size,
	  which is left as it is and counts as none.

	A disk with no RigidDiskBlock of its own whose first block is a
	protective MBR, or whose second begins with a GPT header, holds a GPT
	(`sdk.dos.gpt`):
	- RDBF_GPT: its partitions are on `gpt_partitions`, a GPTPartition per
	  used entry, and its header is `gpt_header` - the one in block 1 with
	  its entries where both are sound, the copy in the last block
	  otherwise, which also sets RDBF_GPTCHANGED.
	- RDBF_GPTDAMAGED: neither copy is sound, and the list is empty.
	- RDBF_CONTAINER: the first of its partitions of the type
	  `gpt.TYPE_CONTAINER` is where the table is looked for, as on a disk
	  of its own: `container` is its first block on the disk, every block
	  number and cylinder of the table counts from there, `geometry`
	  counts the container's blocks and `disk_blocks` the disk's.
	A RigidDiskBlock in the disk's own first blocks is the table even where
	the disk has a GPT too, and the GPT is not read.

	A disk with no table is not a failure - that is how a blank one looks -
	and InitRDB makes it one. The blocks of the file system headers, of
	their code and of the bad-block list are noted, so WriteRDB never puts
	a PartitionBlock over them. `change_num` is the unit's TD_CHANGENUM,
	which says later whether the medium is still this one.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the device.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a Task will do. The handle's reply port is the caller's,
	  so every call with it must come from the same task.

   OWNERSHIP
	The handle, its nodes and the open unit are the caller's until
	CloseRDB.

   NOTES
	The unit stays open for the handle's life; a file system on it is not
	disturbed by that.

   BUGS
	Blocks past the first 256 are not looked at for the kept chains, and
	WriteRDB uses none of them.

   SEE ALSO
	`CloseRDB`, `NextPartition`, `InitRDB`, `WriteRDB`, `NextGPTPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    var err: i32 = 0;
	    const handle = rb.OpenRDB("sdcard.device", 0, &err) orelse return err;
	    defer rb.CloseRDB(handle);
	    if (handle.flags & rdb.RDBF_FOUND == 0) _ = rb.InitRDB(handle);
rdb.library/RemFileSystem                            rdb.library/RemFileSystem

   NAME
	RemFileSystem -- Takes a file system's code out of a handle's table and frees it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemFileSystem(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, file_system: *rdb.RDBFileSystem) void

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -64.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `file_system`: one of its file systems.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	Only the table in memory changes, and RDBF_CHANGED is set: the disk
	keeps the code until WriteRDB writes a table without it, whose blocks
	are then free for the table's own use.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The file system is freed, with the code AddFileSystem copied; a
	pointer to it is no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	A drive mounted with the code keeps running it until the system
	starts again; from then on the drive's handler is the ROM's, or the
	one its DosType names.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddFileSystem`, `NextFileSystem`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.NextFileSystem(handle, null)) |file_system| rb.RemFileSystem(handle, file_system);
	    _ = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/RemGPT                                          rdb.library/RemGPT

   NAME
	RemGPT -- Takes the GPT out of a handle, so that InitRDB makes a table over the whole disk, and WriteRDB wipes the disk's GPT once it has written it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemGPT(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle) void

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -92.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	The GPT partitions are freed, and so is a table in the container with
	its partitions and file systems; the handle has no window, no table,
	and none of RDBF_GPT, RDBF_GPTDAMAGED, RDBF_GPTCHANGED and
	RDBF_CONTAINER. Where the disk holds a GPT, the next WriteRDB wipes it
	after its table is down: the header in block 1, the copy in the last
	block, and the protective MBR, where the new table does not use those
	blocks. A handle without a GPT is left as it is.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The GPT partitions and the dropped table's nodes are freed; a pointer
	to any of them is no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	Every partition of the GPT is lost with it, the PC's and the
	container's, once WriteRDB has run.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`InitRDB`, `WriteRDB`, `InitGPT`

   EXAMPLES
	    rb.RemGPT(handle);
	    if (rb.InitRDB(handle) != rdb.RDBERR_OK) return;
	    _ = rb.AddPartition(handle, "DH1", 0, 0, sdk.dos.posfs.ID_POSFS_DISK);
	    _ = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/RemGPTPartition                        rdb.library/RemGPTPartition

   NAME
	RemGPTPartition -- Takes a partition off a handle's GPT and frees it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemGPTPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, partition: *rdb.GPTPartition) void

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -84.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `partition`: one of the handle's GPT partitions.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	The partition's entry is unused from now on, and RDBF_GPTCHANGED is
	set. Taking out the container the table is in takes the table with
	it, as InitGPT does: its partitions and file systems are freed, and
	the handle has no window and no table. A second container on the GPT
	stays a partition of the GPT to the handle. Only the handle changes;
	WriteGPT puts the change on the disk.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition is freed; a pointer to it is no use afterwards, and so
	are pointers to a dropped table's partitions.

   NOTES
	Nothing on the partition's blocks is touched, and a drive mounted from
	it stays until the system starts again.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddGPTPartition`, `WriteGPT`, `RemGPT`

   EXAMPLES
	    const part = rb.NextGPTPartition(handle, null) orelse return;
	    rb.RemGPTPartition(handle, part);
	    _ = rb.WriteGPT(handle);
rdb.library/RemPartition                              rdb.library/RemPartition

   NAME
	RemPartition -- Takes a partition off a handle's list and frees it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn RemPartition(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle, partition: *rdb.RDBPartition) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB.
	- `partition`: one of its partitions.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	Only the table in memory changes, and RDBF_CHANGED is set: the disk
	keeps the partition until WriteRDB writes a chain without it. Its
	cylinders are free for AddPartition at once; what is on them is not
	touched, by this or by WriteRDB.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The partition is freed; a pointer to it is no use afterwards.

   NOTES
	A partition of another handle must not be given.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddPartition`, `FindPartition`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.FindPartition(handle, "DH1")) |part| rb.RemPartition(handle, part);
	    _ = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/WriteGPT                                      rdb.library/WriteGPT

   NAME
	WriteGPT -- Checks a handle's GPT and writes it to the disk: the copy at the end, then the one at the start, then the protective MBR.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn WriteGPT(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle) i32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -88.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB with a sound GPT, or after InitGPT.

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK when both copies are on the disk. Before anything is
	written: RDBERR_NOGPT for a handle without a sound GPT;
	RDBERR_BLOCKSIZE for a medium whose erase unit is larger than its
	blocks; RDBERR_CHANGED when the medium in the unit is not the one the
	GPT was read from; RDBERR_RANGE for a partition whose blocks are
	outside the usable ones, the wrong way round or another's, or a GPT
	whose copy at the end does not fit before the disk's last block;
	RDBERR_NOMEM. RDBERR_IO when the device refused a write, part of the
	way through.

   BEHAVIOR
	The entries are made from the handle's list, each partition's in its
	place by `number`, every other entry zeroes; both headers carry their
	crc32 and the entries'. The header in block 1 names the copy in the
	disk's last block, its entries follow it from block 2, and the copy's
	entries follow the last usable block. The disk's GUID, its usable
	blocks, the entries' count and size are the handle's `gpt_header`'s,
	as read or as InitGPT made them; a disk that has grown since keeps its
	usable blocks, and the copy moves to its new last block.

	The copy at the end is written first, then the entries and the header
	at the start, so that the disk holds a whole GPT at every point of the
	write (see the top of the file). Block 0 is made a protective MBR
	where it is not one - a hybrid MBR is left as it is - and a
	RigidDiskBlock in the first RDB_LOCATION_LIMIT blocks that the GPT did
	not write over is wiped. A medium that wants erasing has each block
	erased before it is written. CMD_UPDATE ends it.

	Afterwards `gpt_header` is the header in block 1, and RDBF_GPTCHANGED
	is cleared.

	Nothing but the GPT's blocks and those wiped is written: what is on a
	partition's blocks stays.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the device.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the handle.

   OWNERSHIP
	The handle stays the caller's.

   NOTES
	Write the GPT before the table in its container: WriteRDB refuses a
	table in a container that is not on the disk yet (RDBERR_GPT).

	A drive mounted from a partition keeps its blocks until the system
	starts again.

   BUGS
	A cut after the protective MBR and before a RigidDiskBlock in the
	first blocks is wiped leaves the disk read as that table's.

   SEE ALSO
	`InitGPT`, `AddGPTPartition`, `RemGPTPartition`, `WriteRDB`

   EXAMPLES
	    if (rb.WriteGPT(handle) == rdb.RDBERR_OK) _ = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
rdb.library/WriteRDB                                      rdb.library/WriteRDB

   NAME
	WriteRDB -- Checks a handle's table and writes it to the disk: the code of its file systems, a FileSysHeaderBlock per file system, a PartitionBlock per partition, then the RigidDiskBlock.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn WriteRDB(base: *RDBBase, handle: *rdb.RDBHandle) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -48.

   INPUTS
	- `handle`: from OpenRDB, with a table (RDBF_FOUND, or after InitRDB).

   RESULT
	RDBERR_OK when all of it is on the disk. Before anything is written:
	RDBERR_NORDB for a handle with no table; RDBERR_BLOCKSIZE for a medium
	whose erase unit is larger than its blocks, where erasing one block
	would take its neighbours with it; RDBERR_CHANGED when the medium in
	the unit is not the one the table was read from; RDBERR_NAME for a
	partition whose name is empty, too long, holds a colon or a slash, or
	is another's; RDBERR_RANGE for one whose cylinders are outside the
	table's usable ones, the wrong way round or another's; RDBERR_FULL for
	more partitions than the table's own blocks hold, more than 16 file
	systems, or code that does not fit even with the area grown up to the
	first partition; RDBERR_NOMEM; RDBERR_IO when a block of the disk's
	chain of file systems cannot be read; RDBERR_GPT for a table in a
	container whose GPT the handle changed and WriteGPT has not written.
	RDBERR_IO when the device refused a write, part of the way through.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every partition is checked first, as AddPartition checks a new one,
	since the program may have changed any of them. Each then gets a
	block in the table's own area (`rdb_blocks_lo` to `rdb_blocks_hi`),
	never one of the code the table keeps or of the bad-block list, and so
	does the RigidDiskBlock, in one of the first RDB_LOCATION_LIMIT
	blocks. Each file system gets a block for its header, and code that
	AddFileSystem gave a LoadSegBlock per piece: in the area, or past it
	up to the first partition's cylinder (on a table with no partition,
	up to its last cylinder). Blocks the disk's table is not in are taken
	first. Where the code reaches past the area, `rdb_blocks_hi` grows to
	the end of the cylinder it reaches into and `lo_cylinder` to the next
	one; the area never shrinks.

	The new code is written first, as chains of LoadSegBlocks, then the
	FileSysHeaderBlocks in the list's order, chained the same way, then
	the PartitionBlocks in the list's order, chained the same way, each
	with its checksum, then the RigidDiskBlock pointing at the first of
	each; then every other RigidDiskBlock in the first blocks is wiped,
	the one the disk's readers took until now last. A reader takes the
	lowest sound RigidDiskBlock, so whichever of the old and the new is
	lower, the disk holds a whole table at every point of the write: the
	old one until the new replaces it, the new one after. A medium that
	wants erasing has each block erased before it is written, and a wiped
	block erased; on any other a wiped block is written with zeroes.
	CMD_UPDATE ends it, so a device that holds writes back has them on the
	medium when the call returns.

	A table in a GPT's container (RDBF_CONTAINER) is written inside the
	container, every block number counted from its first block. A table
	written after RemGPT on a disk that held a GPT wipes the GPT once the
	table is down: the header in block 1, its copy in the last block and
	the protective MBR, where the table does not use those blocks.

	Afterwards `block` is the RigidDiskBlock's, each partition's and each
	file system's `at` is its block, a file system's `blocks` counts its
	code's, the copies AddFileSystem made are freed, RDBF_FOUND is set and
	RDBF_CHANGED, RDBF_DAMAGED and RDBF_FOREIGN are cleared.

	Nothing but the table's blocks is written: what is on a partition's
	cylinders stays, whatever the table now says of them.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the device.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the handle.

   OWNERSHIP
	The handle stays the caller's.

   NOTES
	A drive mounted from a partition keeps the cylinders it was mounted
	with until the system starts again: changing a partition's cylinders
	or file system under its drive leaves the drive on the old ones.
	Inhibit the drive first, and restart once the table is written.

	After RDBERR_IO the handle no longer says what the disk holds: close
	it, and open the unit again to see.

   BUGS
	Where the table's area has no block to spare, the RigidDiskBlock is
	written over the old one, and on a medium that erases, a cut between
	that block's erase and its write leaves the disk without a table. A
	write that fails after the first PartitionBlock that had to reuse a
	block of the old chain can leave the old table pointing at a new
	block; so can new code that had to go in the old table's blocks. Code
	that goes past the area is written over the first blocks of a
	partition that the old table had there.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRDB`, `InitRDB`, `AddPartition`, `RemPartition`, `MountPartition`

   EXAMPLES
	    const err = rb.WriteRDB(handle);
	    if (err != rdb.RDBERR_OK) _ = Printf(dl, "not written (%ld)\n", .{err});
